# Rosetta Intel per-line skips (RosettaHarnessAlignmentWorkOrder.md §4.3).
# Every port reads this file; no port keeps its own copy.
#
#   =<tag>  <reason>                  defines a reason once
#   line    <tag>  <exact line>       skips the case whose trimmed line text matches
#   attr    <tag>  <ops>  <attribute> skips lines of the comma-separated ops that
#                                     carry the attribute token
#
# Fields are tab-separated (no corpus line contains a tab). Every entry must
# match at least one corpus line (checked by rosetta/check_rosetta.py).
# Transcribed from Swift's RosettaIntelSkips.swift on 2026-11-12.

=fma-qnan	fma: Intel expects a canonical -qNaN(0) that matches neither Intel's first-NaN-wins rule nor GDAS sNaN-prefers
=exp-flags	exp/exp10: Intel marks certain tiny-input results EXACT (flag 00); they are actually inexact
=minmax-qnan	maxnum/maxnum_mag/minnum_mag: a canonical -qNaN(0) case that matches neither Intel's rule nor GDAS
=from-string-malformed	from_string, malformed string: we return NaN and raise invalid (conversion syntax); Intel returns NaN with no flag, and reads the valid prefix of a string with trailing garbage ("1.1E-2E" → 0.011)
=longint32	lrint/lround with a 32-bit `long` result: a 32-bit integer conversion, out of scope like the excluded bid128_to_int32_* ops (the longintsize=64 lines run)
=pow-exact-inexact	pow: Intel flags an exact result inexact (pow(-1, -3) = -1.0 with flag 20); the result is exact
=pow-short-result	pow: Intel's expected value has 20 digits, not 34 (pow(-0.875, -3))
=pow-zero-cohort	pow: a zero power from a non-canonical (zero) x and y = 1.0: Intel gives exponent 0; we keep pown's / the GDAS preferred exponent y·Q(x)
=pow-malformed	pow: a one-operand line (Intel readtest.c splits by token count); not a valid pow case

attr	longint32	bid128_lrint,bid128_lround	longintsize=32
